Two things that may help narrow it.

**Any literal above 256 takes the same path.** `U32.to_nat(0)` is the longhand, but plain literals reach it too, because `nat_to_term` expands a literal up to `NAT_LITERAL_MAX = 256` into a `Succ` chain and anything larger into `U32.to_nat(n)`. On the type from the report:

```
case 255n  ->  "a Zero pattern with 1 field"   caught
case 256n  ->  "a Zero pattern with 1 field"   caught
case 257n  ->  accepted, branch goal becomes @w:U -> U
case 300n  ->  accepted, branch goal becomes @w:U -> U
```

So the checker's verdict on a pattern changes at 256, and an ordinary literal is enough to hit it.

**The two pattern paths differ in `parse_patt` (bend.ts:1727).** The literal branch builds the pattern and returns before reaching the constructor case:

```ts
const lit = t.$ === "App" ? nat_from_term(t) : null;
if (lit !== null) {
  let q: Patt = { $: "PCtr", k: "Zero", x: [], s: t.s };
  for (let i = 0; i < lit; i++) {
    q = { $: "PCtr", k: "Succ", x: [q], s: t.s };
  }
  return q;
}
```

It never calls `book_ctr`, so neither the unknown-constructor check nor the arity check at :1744-1750 runs. Written honestly both checks fire: `case Zero{}:` gives "a Zero pattern with 1 field", and the self-call gives "expected: a decreasing self-call". Only the literal spelling escapes both.

Related: bend.ts:2763 does its own arity check with a different message, so there is more than one place building patterns without the shared check.

I found a stronger closed form of this on current `main` (`6018e28e`, Bend 2.0.21). It removes F32, command-line input and IO from the failure, and puts the expected result in a checked theorem in the same file.

Starting from the file in the issue, replace every `F32` with `U32`, replace both `1.0` literals with `1`, remove `depth.of`, `depth` and the old `main`, then use:

```bend
def run(+d: Nat) -> U32:
  Mat.sum(d, d, Mat.matmul(d, d, d, Mat.full(d, d, 1), Mat.full(d, d, 1)))

def hold(n: Nat) -> Nat:
  n

def opaque_two(u: Unit) -> Nat:
  a b = hold(1n) hold(0n)
  1n+a

def opaque_two_ok() -> {opaque_two(Unit{}) == 2n : Nat}:
  {==}

def runtime_tag() -> Bool:
  U32.is_eq(run(opaque_two(Unit{})), 64)

def runtime_tag_ok() -> {runtime_tag() == True{} : Bool}:
  {==}

def main() -> Bool:
  runtime_tag()
```

The parallel let keeps the closed `2n` opaque to the compiler while remaining definitionally equal to `2n` for the checker. Results:

```text
$ bun bend2/main.ts closed.bend --check-only
All terms check.
$ bun bend2/main.ts closed.bend
True{}
$ bun bend2/main.ts closed.bend -o closed.js && bun closed.js
True{}
$ bun bend2/main.ts closed.bend -o closed && ./closed
False{}
```

Repeated runs of the exact same C binary alternate between `False{}` and `bend: memory fault (machine stack overflow?)` on my M4 Pro. So this is reproducible with a fully closed program, integer leaves and a checked proposition whose runtime value the C backend flips. There are no templates, `@unsafe`, FFI, holes or open user laws involved.

I reduced this to a much smaller closed repro and found the specific missed fact. This is the same root cause as the matrix failure.

```text
$ bun bend2/main.ts nested_family.bend --check-only
All terms check.
$ bun bend2/main.ts nested_family.bend
True{}
$ bun bend2/main.ts nested_family.bend -o out.js && bun out.js
True{}
$ bun bend2/main.ts nested_family.bend -o out && ./out
