```

The result is deterministic on both the 2.0.21 tag and current `main` (`6018e28e`). The file is closed and pure. It has no templates, `@unsafe`, FFI, holes or open user laws. `result_ok` proves `result() == 29`, and `runtime_tag_ok` proves the `True{}` that native C changes to `False{}`.

```bend
import Base

type RT is Data:
  RT{a: U32, b: U32, c: U32}

law F:
  for t: Nat
  for n: Nat
  Data

def F(t, n):
  match t n:
    case 0n 0n:
      U32
    case 0n 1n+p:
      U32
    case 1n+q 0n:
      RT
    case 1n+q 1n+p:
      RT

type Carrier<-n: Nat, -t: Nat> is Data:
  Carrier{payload: F(t, n), blob: String, tail: Maybe<&2, Carrier<n, t>>}

def hold(n: Nat) -> Nat:
  n

def opaque_zero() -> Nat:
  a b = hold(0n) hold(1n)
  a

def make_rt(+n: Nat, +x: U32) -> F(1n, n):
  match n:
    case 0n:
      RT{x, U32.add(x, 1), U32.add(x, 7)}
    case 1n+p:
      RT{x, U32.add(x, 1), U32.add(x, 7)}

def pack(+n: Nat, r: F(1n, n), s: String) -> Carrier<n, 1n>:
  Carrier{r, s, None{}}

def take_blob(c: Carrier<0n, 0n>) -> String:
  Carrier{payload, blob, tail} = c
  blob

def heat_carrier(+c: Carrier<0n, 0n>) -> String:
  String.append(take_blob(c), take_blob(c))

def use(+n: Nat, c: Carrier<n, 1n>) -> U32:
  match n:
    case 0n:
      Carrier{r, blob, tail} = c
      RT{a, b, c2} = r
      U32.add(a, c2)
    case 1n+p:
      Carrier{r, blob, tail} = c
      RT{a, b, c2} = r
      U32.add(a, c2)

def result() -> U32:
  +n = opaque_zero()
  U32.add(U32.from_nat(String.length(heat_carrier(Carrier{0, U32.show(U32.add(U32.from_nat(n), 7)), None{}}))), use(n, pack(n, make_rt(n, 10), U32.show(U32.from_nat(n)))))

def result_ok() -> {result() == 29 : U32}:
  {==}

def runtime_tag() -> Bool:
  U32.is_eq(result(), 29)

def runtime_tag_ok() -> {runtime_tag() == True{} : Bool}:
  {==}

def main() -> Bool:
  runtime_tag()
```

`Carrier<0n, 0n>` is shared only to make its recursive constructor hot at an instantiation whose payload is `U32`. `pack` then builds the same hot constructor at the open type `Carrier<n, 1n>`, whose payload is `F(1n, n)`. `RT` must become hot before that payload is sealed.

The missed fact is in `facts_fam`. For `F(1n, n)`, it calls `mat_arms` only on the outer match over `t`. Those arm bodies are still matches over `n`:

```text
Zero -> match n { Zero -> U32; Succ -> U32 }
Succ -> match n { Zero -> RT;  Succ -> RT  }
```

They reach `facts_hot` as `Mat` terms, and the walk stops one level above `RT`. `CID_CARRIER` is therefore hot while `CID_RT` stays cold. The hot build seals the `RT` payload; `use` takes it as a cold raw three-word record and reads the one-word seal cell as its fields.

A semantic control splits the inner match into `G(n)`. Then `facts_fam` sees `Zero -> RT` and `Succ -> RT`, `CID_RT` becomes hot, and C prints `True{}`. In a temporary copy of the compiler, recursively walking a forced `Mat` inside `facts_hot` also makes this repro print `True{}`; the same change makes the closed U32 matrix repro from my previous comment print `True{}` in 8/8 runs instead of alternating between `False{}` and a memory fault. That ties this directly to #901 rather than a second backend bug.

Both points are in f7acd448 (test and commit message amended in place).

1. The test now builds the list at runtime exactly as you sketched (`wrap` from `List.length` of `IO.args`). Emitting C for it on main vs this branch differs in only the two lines the fix moves:
```c
// main                                // this branch
u64 _nd_2 = _sp_0;                     u64 _nd_2 = heap_alloc(e, cls_fit(2));
u64 _nd_3 = heap_alloc(e, cls_fit(2)); u64 _nd_3 = _sp_0;
```
On main `_sp_0` goes into the Pair node and the Cons rebuild allocates, so the Scalar path orphans the cell; here the Pair allocates and the rebuild keeps `_sp_0`. The header no longer says "Regression" — it is a smoke test, notes a leak is not observable in the output, and that a literal list folds away on both. Interp, C and JS all print `7` / `3`; `tests/run/spare_reuse.bend` still prints `10n` (interp + C); `bun gates/repo.ts` PASS 46 / 46.

2. The commit message now carries the trade-off verbatim: arms no longer see outer spares, so an arm that reused a same-size spare on main now heap_allocs, and the spare comes back only if a same-size build follows, otherwise the next flush frees it.

Thank you, this fixes a real checking failure: the proof of `100000n + 100000n == 200000n` overflows before the PR and checks with it. But I don’t think the current implementation is a good enough trade-off.

Recursive comparison is more susceptible to stack overflow, but it follows the structure of the Lean formalization more directly. The new clause adds a separate path for the last field and repeats evaluation and literal handling there. That makes the comparison harder to audit against the formal rules.

The fix also still depends on field order. This valid proof overflows with the PR:

```python
import Base

type Chain is Data:
  End{}
  Link{next: Chain, value: Nat}

def build(n: Nat) -> Chain:
  match n:
    case Zero{}:
      End{}
    case Succ{p}:
      Link{build(p), 0n}

def same() -> {build(10000n) == build(10000n) : Chain}:
  {==}
```

The first field still takes a recursive call for every link. Moving `next` to the last field makes the proof pass at the same depth.

Some proofs also become slower to check:

```python
import Base

def Goal(+n: Nat) -> Data:
  {Succ{n} == Succ{n} : Nat}

def check() -> Goal(Nat.sub(100000n, 100000n)):
  {==}
```

Both versions accept this proof, but checking the file on my machine took **0.208 s before the PR and 0.397 s with it**, about 1.9 times as long.

Both occurrences of `n` refer to the same stored expression. The existing comparison recognizes that before evaluating it. The new loop evaluates first, so it computes the subtraction unnecessarily.

Given the remaining overflows and this regression, the benefit does not justify the added complexity and the less direct correspondence with the formalization. We can revisit this if a revision offers better trade-offs. Fixing the slowdown alone would not settle this: implementation complexity and ease of comparison with the Lean formalization would still weigh in the decision.

