Hey Adriel, thanks for the contribution. 

The change seems correct, but on the gate's M4 minis it costs tree-matmul about 10% on PAR-GPU. A one-line change to the condition in gpu_run removes that loss and keeps the raytrace gain.

Where tree-matmul loses: almost all of it is one round: 3072 tasks grown into 24576. On an M4 Pro, with one command buffer per kernel, that round's work pass goes from 273 ms to 311 ms. We changed how pass 3 picks its ring and timed the GPU:

<img width="930" height="355" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e5c92573-ee57-41b9-a0f9-e889f38fea2f" />

The stored put costs nothing. Balance across groups doesn't explain it either: the chunked flip balances the groups like ring_flip does, yet it gets both the gain and the loss. What matters is which tasks share a SIMD group.

Why. When several lanes of a grow group fork together, the group's cursor puts kid i of lane L at 24i + L. So neighbouring rings hold the same kid of different parents.

- If the round grew from one root, as raytrace and tree-matmul's first round do, those parents are cousins, and a SIMD group of neighbouring rings runs related work. tree-matmul's first round goes from 15.0 to 3.5 ms.
- If the round starts from deals made through the cursor (f >= CUBE_T), deal c sits in grow group c % CUBE_G. Neighbouring rings then hold deals CUBE_G apart. ring_flip reads one ring position across 32 neighbouring grow groups, which are consecutive deals: the previous round's siblings. In tree-matmul those share their input blocks (mul(a0, b0) and mul(a0, b1)).

Suggested change. Use pass 3 only when the grow on one group ran:

```c
static void gpu_run(u32 f) {
  if (f < CUBE_T) {
    gpu_kernel(0, 1);
  }
  if (f < LANES) {
    gpu_kernel(0, CUBE_G);
  }
  gpu_kernel(f < CUBE_T ? 3 : 1, CUBE_G);
  gpu_kernel(2, 1);
}
```

Pass 3 still runs only right after a grow on CUBE_G groups, so the argument for why it cannot hang is unchanged. A round with CUBE_T <= f < LANES runs as on main. The program in #925 is a single round from f = 1, so it behaves the same as with this PR.

<img width="1420" height="481" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ded08456-cfc5-4258-939f-049a0586ee8a" />

Smaller points:
- Name the stored put. A macro next to the ring macros, e.g. #define ring_held(H, r) (ring_put(H, r) + 1), would document the upper half of the put word instead of the bare + 1 and + (pass == 3).
- Store it only after a grow. Only the value the grow leaves is read, so the store at the end of bend_dev can be if (pass == 0).
- Fix the pass header. It still says pass 1 "drains each lane's ring", but pass 1 drains ring_flip(me). The new sentence should also say pass 3 runs only after a grow from fewer than CUBE_T roots, and the BendRT sentence should match.
- CUBE_LOG on Metal. At d2066a52 it is the default 7 (128 groups, 16384 lanes), not 5 as the description says, so the ring arithmetic there describes CUBE_G 32.

Hello, thanks for the contribution!

The diagnosis is right: rewalking an already-normal tail at each binding is what makes `conversion_data_bound_once` quadratic, and I confirmed the fix on that test (base kernel: out of fuel in 4.1 s; this branch: checks in 0.66 s). The `B` step in `wnf_pars` is fine too. But I found two cases where the new check makes things worse, so I don't think it can go in as is.

**1. `Term.uses` walks the data, outside the fuel budget.** The PR says the check reads "a static term, the def's source, never the data", but `fire` calls it on `f` after `run` has put the environment into the body. So when a recursion's step has a `+` let, the let's body holds the tail, and each step walks the rest of the list. Here is the PR's shape with one let added:

```python
def allone(+xs: List<&2, U32>) -> Bool:
  match xs:
    case Nil{}:
      True{}
    case Con{h, t}:
      +k = U32.is_eq(h, 1)
      Bool.and(k, allone(t))

def both(+xs: List<&2, U32>) -> Bool:
  Bool.and(allone(xs), allone(xs))

def all_n() -> {both(ones(4000n)) == True{} : Bool}:
  {==}
```

On this branch, 500 cells take 0.9 s (0.08 s without the let) and 4000 cells take 53 s, then check (0.58 s without the let). That's 59× the time for 8× the data, so it's still quadratic. The base kernel gives up with "out of fuel" after 5 s, but `Term.uses` spends no fuel, so FUEL no longer bounds `--verdict`'s running time and larger inputs just hang. The comment above `Term.wnf` ("one budget bounds all the work") would no longer be true.

**2. Counting only live uses misses erased copies.** Conversion compares erased positions too: erased fields, erased arguments, `Eql` sides. A `+` binder with one live use and k erased copies used to be normalized once and shared; now each copy is evaluated separately:

```python
type Box is Data:
  B{-f0: Bool, -f1: Bool, -f2: Bool, -f3: Bool, -f4: Bool, -f5: Bool, -f6: Bool, -f7: Bool, v: Bool}

def h2(+d: Nat) -> Bool:
  match d:
    case 0n:
      True{}
    case 1n+j:
      Bool.and(h2(j), h2(j))

def tagit(+x: Bool) -> Box:
  B{x, x, x, x, x, x, x, x, x}

def goal() -> {tagit(h2(21n)) == B{True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}, True{}} : Box}:
  {==}
```

bend2 checks it. The base kernel says ALL PROOFS CHECK in 2.25 s; this branch says `In goal: out of fuel` after 6.75 s. At `h2(18n)` both check, but this branch is 9× slower (0.29 s → 2.60 s). That's a new false reject, and the `tests/` sweep doesn't catch it because nothing there has this shape.

Both point the same way: whether a binder is copied should be decided from the def's source, before anything is substituted in (or once, when the book is checked), and erased uses should count as copies. Putting the check inside `Term.val`, instead of an `if` at two call sites, would also keep it in one place and avoid the new step in `wnf_pars`.

**Housekeeping:**
- It conflicts with main: #1192 rewrote `wnf_pars`, so `B` and its two uses need redoing in the new style.
- `tests/check/conversion_data_bound_once.bend` expects `ALL PROOFS CHECK`, which is plain `bend`'s output, so `gates/test.ts` passes it on main as well. Only `gates/safe.ts tests` checks the fix.
- Bench (`bench/checker` exported to BendTT, kernel time, median of 5 interleaved runs, on an M-series laptop): I see `trees_400` +1.9%, matching yours, but `generics_3200` is +0.2% for me, not −5%. The rest are within ±1%.

